## 1. Architecture

### 1.1 What is an MCU (Microcontroller Unit)?

An **MCU (Microcontroller Unit)** is a **single-chip computer** designed for embedded systems. It combines everything a computer needs onto one chip:

```
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    MCU (Microcontroller Unit)                            │
│                    Example: STM32F407VGTx                                │
│                                                                          │
│   ┌──────────────┐   ┌────────────┐   ┌────────────┐   ┌────────────┐    │
│   │  CPU Core    │   │   Flash    │   │    SRAM    │   │    DMA     │    │
│   │  (Cortex-M4) │   │  (1 MB)    │   │  (192 KB)  │   │ Controller │    │
│   │  ← The Brain │   │  ← Program │   │  ← Working │   │ ← Auto     │    │
│   │    of the    │   │    storage │   │    memory  │   │   data     │    │
│   │    chip      │   │            │   │            │   │   transfer │    │
│   └──────┬───────┘   └──────┬─────┘   └──────┬─────┘   └──────┬─────┘    │
│          │                  │                │                │          │
│          └──────────────────┴────────────────┴────────────────┘          │
│                              │ BUS MATRIX │                              │
│          ┌──────────────────┬┴────────────┴┬─────────────────┐           │
│          │                  │              │                 │           │
│   ┌──────┴──────┐    ┌──────┴──────┐  ┌────┴─────┐    ┌──────┴──────┐    │
│   │    GPIO     │    │    UART     │  │   ADC    │    │   Timers    │    │
│   │  (Pins)     │    │  SPI / I2C  │  │   DAC    │    │    PWM      │    │
│   │  Digital    │    │  Comms      │  │  Analog  │    │  Counters   │    │
│   │  I/O        │    │  Interfaces │  │  Convert │    │             │    │
│   └─────────────┘    └─────────────┘  └──────────┘    └─────────────┘    │
│                                                                          │
│   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│   │                    Physical Pins (LQFP100)                       │   │
│   │   PA0  PA1  PA2 ... PB0  PB1 ... PC12  PD2 ... PE0  PE1 ...      │   │
│   │   (100 pins connecting the MCU to the outside world)             │   │
│   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

**Think of it like a human body:**

| Human Body | MCU (STM32F407) | What It Does |
|---|---|---|
| **Brain** | CPU core (Cortex-M4) | Thinks, makes decisions, executes instructions |
| **Long-term memory** | Flash (1 MB) | Stores your firmware program (survives power-off) |
| **Short-term memory** | SRAM (192 KB) | Holds variables while running (lost on power-off) |
| **Nervous system** | Bus Matrix (AHB/APB) | Connects the brain to all body parts |
| **Eyes, ears** | ADC, GPIO Inputs | Reads sensors, buttons, switches |
| **Hands, muscles** | GPIO Outputs, PWM, DAC | Controls lights, motors, relays, buzzers |
| **Voice** | UART, SPI, I2C | Communicates with other devices |
| **Reflexes** | NVIC (Interrupt Controller) | React instantly to urgent events |
| **Heartbeat** | Timers, SysTick | Keeps time, generates periodic signals |

---

### 1.2 CPU vs MCU — What's the Difference?

These are **not** the same thing. The CPU is a **component inside** the MCU:

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                  MCU (the whole chip)                   │
│                  Example: STM32F407VGTx                 │
│                                                         │
│   ┌───────────────────────────────────┐                 │
│   │         CPU (the brain)           │                 │
│   │         Cortex-M4                 │                 │
│   │                                   │                 │
│   │   Executes your C code            │                 │
│   │   (one instruction at a time)     │                 │
│   └───────────────────────────────────┘                 │
│                                                         │
│   + Flash memory (stores your firmware)                 │
│   + SRAM (stores your variables)                        │
│   + GPIO (digital I/O pins)                             │
│   + UART, SPI, I2C (communication)                      │
│   + ADC, DAC (analog conversion)                        │
│   + Timers (PWM, counting, timing)                      │
│   + DMA (direct memory access)                          │
│   + Clock system (oscillators, PLL)                     │
│   + NVIC (interrupt management)                         │
│   + Power management                                    │
│                                                         │
│   All of these are NOT the CPU.                         │
│   They are peripherals that surround the CPU.           │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

| | CPU | MCU |
|---|---|---|
| **What is it?** | Just the processing core | The entire chip (CPU + everything else) |
| **Analogy** | The brain only | The entire body (brain + organs + limbs) |
| **Example** | ARM Cortex-M4 | STM32F407VGTx |
| **Can work alone?** | No — needs memory, I/O, clock | Yes — has everything on one chip |
| **Who designs it?** | ARM Ltd. designs the CPU core | ST, NXP, TI, etc. build the MCU around it |

> **Key insight:** ARM Ltd. designs the Cortex-M4 **CPU core blueprint** and licenses it. Then chip manufacturers like **STMicroelectronics** (STM32), **NXP** (Kinetis), and **TI** (Tiva-C) each build their own MCU around that same CPU core, adding their own choice of Flash, RAM, and peripherals. That's why different MCU brands can have the same Cortex-M4 CPU but different peripherals.

---

### 1.3 What is Cortex-M4?

**ARM Cortex-M4** is the **name of the CPU core** — the processing engine inside the MCU. It is:

- Designed by **ARM Ltd.** (a company that designs CPU architectures)
- Part of the **Cortex-M family** (a family of CPU cores optimized for microcontrollers)
- A **32-bit** processor (works with 32-bit data and addresses)
- The **brain** that actually executes your firmware code

**Cortex-M4 exists as a separate core inside the MCU**, surrounded by peripherals:

```
Inside the STM32F407VGTx chip
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

                ┌─────────────────────────────────┐
                │       ARM Cortex-M4 Core        │  ← CPU (the brain)
                │                                 │
                │  ┌─────┐ ┌─────┐ ┌──────────┐   │
                │  │ ALU │ │ FPU │ │ Registers│   │
                │  └─────┘ └─────┘ │  R0–R15  │   │
                │  ┌─────────────┐ └──────────┘   │
                │  │   NVIC      │ ┌──────────┐   │
                │  │ (Interrupt  │ │ SysTick  │   │
                │  │  Controller)│ │ Timer    │   │
                │  └─────────────┘ └──────────┘   │
                └────────────┬────────────────────┘
                             │
                    ═════════╪═══════════  BUS MATRIX
                    ║        ║          ║
              ┌─────╨─────┐ ┌╨──────┐  ┌╨──────────────────┐
              │   Flash   │ │ SRAM  │  │   Peripherals     │
              │   1 MB    │ │192 KB │  │ GPIO UART ADC TIM │
              └───────────┘ └───────┘  │ SPI I2C DMA DAC   │
                                       └───────────────────┘

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
         Flash, SRAM, and peripherals are NOT part of
         the Cortex-M4. They surround it and connect
         to it through the bus matrix.
```

### 1.4 The Role of Cortex-M4 in STM32

The Cortex-M4 is the **brain** of the STM32. Here's exactly what it does:

**Yes — the Cortex-M4 executes ALL code:**
- ✅ Your application firmware (FreeRTOS tasks, UART handlers, GPIO control)
- ✅ The bootloader (it's also just code stored in Flash)
- ✅ Interrupt Service Routines (ISRs)
- ✅ The FreeRTOS scheduler
- ✅ HAL library functions

**The complete path from C code to hardware execution:**

```
     You write C code
           │
           ▼
┌─────────────────────────┐
│  main.c                 │
│  HAL_GPIO_WritePin(...) │
│  set_temp_control()     │
│  osDelay(1)             │
└────────────┬────────────┘
             │  ARM GCC Compiler compiles it
             ▼
┌─────────────────────────┐
│  Machine Code (.elf)    │
│  Thumb-2 instructions   │
│  Example: 0xF04F 0x0001 │  ← Binary numbers the CPU understands
└────────────┬────────────┘
             │  ST-Link flashes it
             ▼
┌─────────────────────────┐
│  Flash Memory           │
│  Address: 0x0800_0000   │  ← Stored permanently in the MCU
└────────────┬────────────┘
             │  On power-on, Cortex-M4 starts
             │  reading instructions from Flash
             ▼
┌─────────────────────────┐
│  Cortex-M4 CPU          │
│  Fetches instruction    │  ← Reads from Flash
│  Decodes instruction    │  ← Figures out what to do
│  Executes instruction   │  ← Does it (set GPIO, add numbers, etc.)
│  Repeats forever        │
└─────────────────────────┘
```

> **Simple summary:** You write C → compiler converts to machine code → stored in Flash → Cortex-M4 reads and executes it, one instruction at a time, forever.

---

